---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { appStoreURL, pageURL, repositoryURL, supportEmail } from '../lib/site';
---
<SiteLayout title="Support — Omnibar for Safari" description="Set up Omnibar 1.2 for Mac and learn about version 2’s native Mac toolbar and keyboard palette for Mac and iPad." section="support">
  <div class="document wrap">
    <header class="document-heading"><p class="eyebrow">A little help</p><h1>Make yourself<br /><em>at home.</em></h1><p>Setup, search settings, and answers to the everyday questions.</p><a class="text-link" href={`mailto:${supportEmail}`}>Email {supportEmail} <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></header>
    <div class="document-body">
      <section id="setup"><h2>Set up Omnibar</h2>
        <p><strong>Version 1.2 is the current public Mac release.</strong> It requires macOS 13 or later and Safari. Version 2 for Mac and iPad is being prepared; its additional setup instructions appear <a href="#version-two">below</a>.</p>
        <ol>
          <li>{appStoreURL ? <span><a href={appStoreURL}>Download Omnibar from the Mac App Store</a>, then open Omnibar.</span> : <span>Open the Omnibar app on your Mac. {repositoryURL && <span>If you’re building it yourself, follow the <a href={repositoryURL}>instructions in the source repository</a>.</span>}</span>}</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Open Safari Settings</strong>. In Safari’s <strong>Extensions</strong> settings, enable Omnibar.</li>
          <li>Allow website access for the sites where you want Omnibar to show the current address. You can also allow it on all websites.</li>
          <li>Click Omnibar in Safari’s toolbar. If the button is hidden, choose <strong>View → Customize Toolbar</strong> in Safari and add it.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Click the toolbar button, type an address or search, and press <kbd>Return</kbd>. Use <kbd>⌘</kbd><kbd>Return</kbd> for a new tab or <kbd>Escape</kbd> to close the popover.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="version-two"><h2>Version 2: Mac toolbar and keyboard palette</h2><p><strong>These features are being prepared for release.</strong> A version 2 build contains two Mac extensions, enabled separately in <strong>Safari → Settings → Extensions</strong>. You can use either or both.</p><ol><li>Open the Omnibar app and choose <strong>Set Up Omnibar…</strong> to enable the native toolbar palette. Its toolbar button handles addresses, searches, local tab matching, and available browser commands.</li><li>Choose <strong>Set Up Omnibar Commands…</strong> to enable the centered keyboard palette. On a normal webpage, press <kbd>⌘</kbd><kbd>⇧</kbd><kbd>P</kbd>. Commands has no toolbar button or popup.</li><li>Allow each extension in Private Browsing separately if you want to use it there.</li></ol><p>The keyboard palette cannot appear on Safari’s Start Page and other restricted pages. Open a normal webpage, or use the native Mac toolbar button.</p></section>

      <section id="ipad"><h2>Version 2: iPad setup</h2><p>The planned iPad version requires <strong>iPadOS 17 or later and a connected hardware keyboard</strong>. It contains the Omnibar Commands web extension and a native settings app.</p><ol><li>Open the Omnibar app and choose <strong>Set Up in Safari…</strong>. In iPad Settings, enable Omnibar Commands under <strong>Apps → Safari → Extensions</strong>. On older iPadOS versions, Safari is directly in Settings.</li><li>Connect a hardware keyboard, open a normal webpage in Safari, and press <kbd>⌘</kbd><kbd>⇧</kbd><kbd>P</kbd>.</li><li>To change engines, the website suffix, or an optional Kagi private link, open the Omnibar app from the Home Screen.</li></ol><p>The iPad palette opens through the keyboard shortcut. It has no toolbar popup, and the native Safari toolbar extension is available only on Mac. Safari controls shortcut availability and conflicts.</p></section>

      <section><h2>Using the version 2 palette</h2><p>Type an address or search, or use <code>@</code> to match tabs and <code>&gt;</code> to find commands. Matching happens locally and stays within the Safari window that opened the palette. Use <kbd>↑</kbd> and <kbd>↓</kbd> to select a result, then <kbd>Return</kbd> to open it. <kbd>⌘</kbd><kbd>Return</kbd> opens an address or search in a new tab.</p><p>Close the centered palette with <kbd>Escape</kbd>, <kbd>⌘</kbd><kbd>⇧</kbd><kbd>P</kbd>, or a click or tap outside it. The Mac toolbar popover also closes with <kbd>Escape</kbd>. You can restore the introduction with <strong>Show Palette Tips Again</strong> in the Omnibar app.</p><p>Start a website with a dot for a shorter address: <code>.cnn</code> expands to <code>https://www.cnn.com</code>, and <code>.bsky.app</code> keeps its supplied suffix. In <strong>Search Settings → Websites</strong>, change the default suffix to another value such as <code>.co.uk</code>.</p></section>

      <section><h2>Why does it ask for website access?</h2><p>Safari requires website access before it supplies a page’s address to the native Mac extension. Omnibar uses that permission to show the URL. The native toolbar extension does not inject scripts or styles, read page contents, or collect browsing history.</p><p>If you don’t grant access, you can still type a destination or search. Some Safari pages, including the Start Page, may not provide an address.</p><p>Version 2’s keyboard palette uses temporary access when you invoke its shortcut to display an extension-owned frame on the page. It does not read the website’s text or forms, save your tab list or unsubmitted input, or send browsing data to the developer.</p></section>

      <section><h2>Can the field stay in the toolbar?</h2><p>Safari’s extension APIs provide a toolbar button that can open a popover. They do not provide a permanently editable address field or another toolbar row. Omnibar shows the current URL while its popover is open.</p><p>Safari’s tab sidebar, Compact tab layout, and <kbd>⌘</kbd><kbd>L</kbd> shortcut continue to work independently.</p></section>

      <section id="search"><h2>Choose and order search engines</h2><p>The search menu in the popover chooses an engine for the current search. Click the gear button to open <strong>Search Settings</strong>. In version 2, settings are also available directly in the Mac app with <kbd>⌘</kbd><kbd>,</kbd>; on iPad, open the Omnibar app.</p><p>On Mac, under <strong>Search Engines</strong>, drag an engine to move it, or use the up and down buttons. On iPad, tap an engine to make it the default, or choose <strong>Edit</strong> to reorder the list. <strong>The first engine is the default.</strong> All seventeen built-in engines are available in every region.</p><p>On first use, your device’s Region setting puts Naver first in South Korea; Baidu, Sogou, and 360 Search first in China; Yandex first in Russia; Seznam first in Czechia; and Yahoo Japan first in Japan. Other regions start with Google. This uses a local setting, with no location request or network lookup. Later region changes do not replace your saved order.</p><p>Omnibar does not synchronize its selection with Safari’s search engine setting. Version 2 shares settings locally between its app and Safari extensions; you can configure them without enabling an extension.</p></section>

      <section><h2>Add a custom engine</h2><ol><li>On Mac, in <strong>Search Settings → Search Engines</strong>, choose <strong>Add → Custom Search Engine…</strong>. In version 2 on iPad, choose <strong>Add Search Engine… → Custom Search Engine…</strong> in the Omnibar app.</li><li>Enter a name and an HTTP or HTTPS search URL containing one <code>%s</code> or <code>{'{searchTerms}'}</code> placeholder.</li><li>Save the engine. It moves to the top of your list and becomes the default.</li></ol><p>For example: <code class="breakable">https://example.com/search?q=%s</code>. On Mac, select a custom engine and choose <strong>Edit</strong> or <strong>Remove</strong> to change it. On iPad, use its information button to edit it, or <strong>Edit</strong> to remove an engine. Use ordinary search URLs here; custom templates are saved in local preferences and should not contain credentials.</p></section>

      <section><h2>Remove and restore engines</h2><p>Select any built-in or custom engine and click <strong>Remove</strong>. Removing the first engine makes the next one the default. At least one engine must remain.</p><p>Removed built-in engines appear in the <strong>Add</strong> menu. Choose one to add it back at the end of your list, keeping your current default and order.</p><p><strong>Restore Defaults</strong> on Mac, or <strong>Restore Built-in Engines</strong> on iPad, brings back all built-in engines in the original order for your device’s current region, followed by your custom engines in their existing relative order. Your custom engine definitions and saved Kagi private session link are kept.</p></section>

      <section id="kagi"><h2>Use Kagi in Private Browsing</h2><p>Regular Kagi searches use your existing Safari sign-in. For private windows, you can optionally save a <a href="https://help.kagi.com/kagi/privacy/private-browser-sessions.html">Kagi private session link</a> in <strong>Search Settings → Kagi Private Browsing</strong>.</p><p>This section appears only while Kagi is in your engine list. Removing Kagi hides it and clears any unsaved input. Adding Kagi back makes the section available again; any previously saved link is kept.</p><p>Paste the link into the secure field and click <strong>Save Link</strong>. It is stored in the device’s local Keychain, is not displayed again, and can be deleted with <strong>Remove Link</strong>. Use this field instead of putting a session token in a custom engine URL.</p><p>Omnibar uses the link only for Kagi searches when Safari confirms that the current page is in Private Browsing. When that information is unavailable, it uses the regular Kagi URL. Direct addresses and other search engines do not read the credential.</p><p>A Kagi session link grants access to your account. Never include it in a support email, screenshot, or public issue. If the link stops working, obtain a new one from Kagi and save it again.</p></section>

      <section><h2>Upgrading from version 1.2 on Mac</h2><p>In version 2, open the native Omnibar toolbar button once to import your existing engine preferences. This preserves newer choices already saved in the app’s shared settings. To import an older saved Kagi private link, open <strong>Search Settings → Kagi Private Browsing</strong> in the native toolbar extension. You can also configure new settings and save a new link directly in the Omnibar app.</p></section>

      <section id="source"><h2>Free and open source</h2><p>Omnibar is free software released under the BSD 2-Clause license. {repositoryURL ? <span>You can <a href={repositoryURL}>read the source, build it, or report an issue on GitHub</a>.</span> : <span>Contact support for source and build information.</span>}</p><p>The app requires no Omnibar account or subscription. A search provider may have its own account requirements or pricing.</p></section>

      <section class="contact-section"><h2>Still need a hand?</h2><p>Email <a href={`mailto:${supportEmail}`}>{supportEmail}</a>. Include your Omnibar, macOS or iPadOS, and Safari versions, what you expected to happen, and what happened instead. For keyboard issues, include your keyboard layout. Please remove personal URLs and credentials from screenshots.</p><p>You can read how the app handles information in the <a href={pageURL('privacy')}>privacy policy</a>.</p></section>
    </div>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
